    "category": "Coding",
    "question": "Write a function to find the longest common subsequence of two strings. Explain your approach and analyze its complexity.",
    "answer_a": "Here is a dynamic programming solution:\n\n```python\ndef lcs(s1, s2):\n    m, n = len(s1), len(s2)\n    dp = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(m + 1)]\n    for i in range(1, m + 1):\n        for j in range(1, n + 1):\n            if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]:\n                dp[i][j] = dp[i-1][j-1] + 1\n            else:\n                dp[i][j] = max(dp[i-1][j], dp[i][j-1])\n    result = []\n    i, j = m, n\n    while i > 0 and j > 0:\n        if s1[i-1] == s2[j-1]:\n            result.append(s1[i-1])\n            i -= 1\n            j -= 1\n        elif dp[i-1][j] > dp[i][j-1]:\n            i -= 1\n        else:\n            j -= 1\n    return ''.join(reversed(result))\n```\n\n**Approach**: Bottom-up dynamic programming. Complexity is O(m * n) time and space.",
    "answer_b": "The longest common subsequence can be found using recursion:\n\n```python\ndef lcs(s1, s2):\n    if not s1 or not s2:\n        return \"\"\n    if s1[-1] == s2[-1]:\n        return lcs(s1[:-1], s2[:-1]) + s1[-1]\n    else:\n        r1 = lcs(s1[:-1], s2)\n        r2 = lcs(s1, s2[:-1])\n        return r1 if len(r1) > len(r2) else r2\n```\n\nThis recursive check checks if the last characters match and takes the max path. Time complexity is O(2^n).",
    "human_preference": "A"
  },
